### November 1 real-server validation

The tuple failure reproduced through the real chat-completions server at `9f921e85398e518c87d621b6ea4582e11966da51` and disappeared at `e6d7f2d7ab92bc6bb9aa23cd1450c67115244f8f`. The full Python source exports differ by the one tuple-unpacking line. Both arms used the same cached Qwen3.8-27B-MTP-NVFP4 weights, tokenizer, runtime and request bytes on an RTX 5090.

| Generated arguments suffix | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Complete `{"s": "hello"}` call | Valid JSON | Valid JSON |
| Open string `{"s": "hello` | Valid JSON | Valid JSON |
| Trailing comma `{"s": "hello", ` | SSE tuple `TypeError` | `{"s": "hello"}` |
| Number then comma `{"n": 42, ` | SSE tuple `TypeError` | `{"n": 42}` |
| Partial literal `{"s": "hello", "n": tr` | SSE tuple `TypeError` | `{"s": "hello"}` |

Each arm received five sequential `POST /v1/chat/completions` requests. All ten HTTP responses were 200. The three baseline failures were **errors inside SSE**, with no terminal finish reason; `[DONE]` alone did not mean success. Every fixed response contained one `f` tool call with the expected JSON, `finish_reason: "tool_calls"`, `[DONE]`, and no error. The two controls passed on both revisions. The baseline server recorded three tracebacks through `chat_completion_stream_generator -> parse_delta -> _flush_arg_converter -> _json_prefix_terminator`, ending with `TypeError: 'in <string>' requires string as left operand, not bool`. The fixed server recorded no traceback.

This uses the public `structured_outputs.choice` API to constrain actual model generation to a Granite tool-call prefix followed by each suffix. It tests deterministic serving integration through the shared parser engine. It does not measure natural tool-call accuracy, an unconstrained truncation rate, client-disconnect handling, or model quality.

Python 3.12.13; torch 2.14.0+cu130; Transformers 5.19.0; FlashInfer 0.7.2; Xgrammar 0.2.8. Exact PR Python source reused the existing `0.32.0rc1.dev326+g7b6310c10` wheel's compiled extensions and packaged support files. This was **not a rebuild of the PR** and does not certify its pinned torch 2.13.0 / FlashInfer 0.7.0.post1 environment. Both arms used `--linear-backend cutlass --attention-backend TRITON_ATTN --enforce-eager`, with `FLASHINFER_DISABLE_JIT=1` and offline Hub loading. No model/dependency download or install was performed.

The completed A/B ran from 15:44:27 to 15:46:03 UTC in 95.4 seconds. Peak sampled GPU memory was 26,328 MiB, temperature 53C, and aggregate owned RSS 19,489,865,728 bytes. Both server exits were 0. Three earlier startup attempts are retained: missing packaged wheel support, an unexpected FlashInfer JIT build stopped at the 48 GiB sampled RSS limit (52,242,972,672 bytes observed), and a rejected CLI spelling. These are not passing runs. The completed experiment used the native JIT-disable setting; the resource limits were not relaxed.

Post-run checks matched 7,717 archived source files in each arm, 2,146 runtime/support files and all 16 cached model files to their recorded hashes. All five request hashes matched across arms, and every saved SSE hash matched its receipt. Cleanup confirmed no GPU compute processes or remaining test listener.

The original full parser run is still **6,208 passed / 19 failed / 297 errors**, exit 1. The later **23 local + 293 remote** assertion passes remain separate; all four remote guard exits **86** remain recorded. This server experiment changes none of those outcomes.

The head is still mergeable. All four inline review threads remain resolved. Hosted contributor eligibility, human submitter review and an upstream `LLM`/`RemoteOpenAIServer` regression selected by CI remain open gates. This experiment invokes the real CLI and HTTP endpoint directly; it is not a claim that such a CI regression has been added or run. No re-review ping or CI-gate change accompanies this evidence update.

